        {
          "id": "21bf4434-5e74-3ac6-8636-3a6e4bbf4164",
          "content": {
            "id": "21bf4434-5e74-3ac6-8636-3a6e4bbf4164",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "UK's Lloyds to put thousands of staff at risk of dismissal, source",
            "description": "<p>STORY: Around 3,000 people at Britain's Lloyds Banking Group, judged to be among the bottom 5%, will find themselves considered for possible dismissal.</p><p>That's according to a source familiar with the matter.</p><p>They added that Lloyds expects about half that number will lose their jobs unless their work improves</p><p>CEO Charlie Nunn is pursuing cost cuts.</p><p>Along with other banks in Britain, Lloyds has reduced its high street presence.</p><p>In January it said it would shut 136 branches as more customers take up digital banking.</p><p>But that it would not directly result in job cuts.</p><p>Economic uncertainty has also made workers cautious about changing jobs.</p><p>The Financial Times newspaper reported that Lloyds employees have been leaving at an annual rate of 5% each year compared to an average of closer to 15% historically.</p><p>A Lloyds spokesperson told Reuters in an emailed statement that it was transforming its business and \"striving to embed a high-performance culture\".</p>",
            "summary": "STORY: Around 3,000 people at Britain's Lloyds Banking Group, judged to be among the bottom 5%, will find themselves considered for possible dismissal.That's according to a source familiar with the matter.They added that Lloyds expects about half that number will lose their jobs unless their work improvesCEO Charlie Nunn is pursuing cost cuts.Along with other banks in Britain, Lloyds has reduced its high street presence.In January it said it would shut 136 branches as more customers take up digital banking.But that it would not directly result in job cuts.Economic uncertainty has also made workers cautious about changing jobs.The Financial Times newspaper reported that Lloyds employees have been leaving at an annual rate of 5% each year compared to an average of closer to 15% historically.A Lloyds spokesperson told Reuters in an emailed statement that it was transforming its business and \"striving to embed a high-performance culture\".",
